  2705	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  2706	# PLAN-135 W2 (unit h8) — idempotent settings-merge: register framework hook
  2707	# registrations into the adopter's EXISTING .claude/settings.json.
  2708	#
  2709	# WHY THIS EXISTS (constraint b, debate R1): install.sh EXISTS-SKIPs an
  2710	# existing settings.json, so a hook that is only baked into the fresh-install
  2711	# template (settings.base.json) NEVER reaches the S217 population of existing
  2712	# adopters. Without this step every hook added after an adopter's original
  2713	# install would be a silent no-op for that repo — the script on disk, the
  2714	# channel unwired. We therefore merge the missing registration(s) into the
  2715	# live settings.json here, at upgrade time, in the SAME ceremony.
  2716	#
  2717	# PLAN-169 W-E (S329) — THE LIST IS DERIVED, NEVER DECLARED TWICE.
  2718	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  2719	# Until S329 this function carried a LITERAL roster of six lifecycle hooks
  2720	# (PreCompact/PostCompact/ConfigChange/SubagentStart/Setup + the PLAN-179 W1-b
  2721	# SessionStart(compact) pinning hook) inside its jq program, and repeated the
  2722	# same six in prose for the --dry-run announcement. That is a second
  2723	# declaration of a truth that already lives in
  2724	# templates/settings/settings.base.json, and it rotted exactly the way a
  2725	# second copy always does: check_ledger_checkpoint.py (PLAN-179 W2/W4) landed
  2726	# in BOTH .claude/settings.json and the template mirror and in NEITHER list,
  2727	# so every adopter that UPGRADED got the hook script with no registration
  2728	# (fresh installs were fine — the template covers them — and
  2729	# test_template_dogfood_parity is blind to it because it compares dogfood to
  2730	# template, never dogfood to the RESULT OF AN UPGRADE). Measured S328, rail
  2731	# codex round 3 of the PLAN-179 pack D; the finding is
  2732	# .claude/plans/PLAN-179/s328-ceremony-D/FINDING-upgrade-lifecycle-hooks-S328.md
  2733	#
  2734	# The cure is NOT a seventh entry. The roster is now DERIVED, per upgrade run,
  2735	# from the template that ships in the SOURCE checkout — the same artifact and
  2736	# the same $SOURCE_DIR resolution the PLAN-164 W1 pair-rail timeout migration
  2737	# already uses (see _migrate_settings_baseline: "the NEW cap is derived from
  2738	# templates/settings/settings.base.json, never hardcoded"). Add a hook to the
  2739	# template and upgraded adopters get it; there is no second place to forget.
  2740	#
  2741	# SOURCE (single):  $SOURCE_DIR/templates/settings/settings.base.json — the
  2742	#   checkout that is EXECUTING this upgrade. Never $TARGET (that is the thing
  2743	#   being upgraded; reading it would make the merge a fixpoint on the
  2744	#   adopter's own drift).
  2745	#
  2746	# SEMANTICS (ADDITIVE — deliberate change from the pre-S329 six):
  2747	#   registration ABSENT for that event  -> the template block is APPENDED
  2748	#   registration PRESENT for that event -> the adopter's block is PRESERVED
  2749	#                                          BYTE-IDENTICAL, never rewritten
  2750	#   Pre-S329 the six were RE-CANONICALIZED: an adopter who had edited one got
  2751	#   it silently replaced. That contradicts INV-4 of PLAN-167/168 (adopter
  2752	#   edits are preserved; REPAIRING a drifted registration is doctor.sh's job,
  2753	#   not the upgrader's), and it does not scale: re-canonicalizing all 47
  2754	#   template registrations would clobber every timeout an adopter ever tuned.
  2755	#   Additive is the only semantics that is safe at the full-roster scale.
  2756	#
  2757	# IDENTITY of a registration (the key the presence test compares):
  2758	#   every "<name>.py" token in the block's hooks[].command, matched as a WHOLE
  2759	#   token — the character class stops at "/" so a path yields its basename,
  2760	#   and a trailing lookahead means "check_x.py" can never be found inside
  2761	#   "check_xy.py" / "check_xy.python". A block whose command carries no .py at
  2762	#   all (the inline PostToolUse|Agent file-assignment echo) is keyed by its
  2763	#   FULL command string. A block is "already registered" under an event when
  2764	#   EVERY key it carries is already present under that event. A block we
  2765	#   cannot key at all (no hooks[]) is never appended — unidentifiable input is
  2766	#   left alone rather than guessed at.
  2767	#
  2768	# --dry-run announces the SAME derivation (one jq program, --arg mode): a
  2769	# migration that is silent in --dry-run is a migration the adopter cannot
  2770	# review before it runs for real.
  2771	#
  2772	# The file is rewritten ONLY when at least one registration is actually
  2773	# missing (same idempotency oracle as the T5.4 migration below), so a re-run
  2774	# is byte-identical — including the adopter's own formatting, which the
  2775	# pre-S329 unconditional `jq` rewrite destroyed on every upgrade.
  2776	#
  2777	# ABSENT vs PRESENT-BUT-FALSY (rail round 1, P2 — these are NOT the same
  2778	# question, and the obvious jq idiom conflates them):
  2779	#   .hooks / an event key MISSING       -> nothing was ever written there;
  2780	#                                          this is the historical adopter, and
  2781	#                                          registering is the whole point
  2782	#   present with an EXPLICIT null/false -> the adopter wrote that on purpose;
  2783	#                                          PRESERVED untouched + named on
  2784	#                                          stderr, exactly like {"not":"array"}
