  2786	# `has(...)` on the CONTAINER. Getting this wrong meant an adopter who had
  2787	# deliberately emptied .hooks got 47 registrations written back into it.
  2788	#
  2789	# THE TWO INPUTS ARE NOT TRUSTED THE SAME WAY (rail round 2, P2):
  2790	#   the ADOPTER's settings.json is untrusted and possibly hand-edited, so a
  2791	#     shape we cannot parse is PRESERVED and NAMED, per EVENT — the other 46
  2792	#     registrations still land (E.8/E.10 of the e2e)
  2793	#   the TEMPLATE is the artifact that DEFINES the roster; it ships with the
  2794	#     framework, and a malformed one means we do not know what the right
  2795	#     answer IS. There is no partial merge to salvage: every event value must
  2796	#     be an array of blocks or the whole merge is REFUSED, named, and nothing
  2797	#     is written. Degrading to "merge the events that happen to parse" would
  2798	#     silently ship a truncated roster, which is this wave's own bug class.
  2799	#
  2800	# Fail-open per CLAUDE.md §5: no jq, unreadable/invalid/structurally-invalid
  2801	# template, malformed settings, or a merge error => stderr NOTE + the upgrade
  2802	# proceeds. A structure we do not understand (settings.json not an object,
  2803	# $.hooks present and not an object, or one event's value present and not an
  2804	# array) is SKIPPED and NAMED WITH ITS TYPE, never coerced. The merged document is
  2805	# validated as JSON and non-empty BEFORE the atomic same-directory mv, so a
  2806	# partial settings.json is never observable. A backup of the pre-merge
  2807	# settings.json is written under $BAK_DIR first so the Owner can always roll
  2808	# back. Suppress entirely with --no-settings-merge.
  2809	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  2810	_merge_lifecycle_hooks_into_settings() {
  2811	  [[ "$SETTINGS_MERGE" -eq 1 ]] || return 0
  2812	  local settings="$TARGET/.claude/settings.json"
  2813	  # SINGLE SOURCE. $SOURCE_DIR is the checkout executing this upgrade (:96),
  2814	  # the same resolution _migrate_settings_baseline uses for the pair-rail cap.
  2815	  #
  2816	  # WHICH template is the CEREMONY's call, and the call is not re-made here
  2817	  # (rail round 6, P1). install.sh builds the adopter's settings.json from
  2818	  # settings.user.json under `--ceremony user` (its WS4-ceremony-settings
  2819	  # branch) — a profile that deliberately OMITS the governance hooks that block
  2820	  # edits or need GPG/sentinel infrastructure. Deriving from settings.base.json
  2821	  # regardless of ceremony would re-register exactly those on the next upgrade,
  2822	  # silently turning the advisory user profile into the maintainer profile —
  2823	  # wider than the pre-cure literal roster ever reached, and in the one
  2824	  # population that chose not to have them. CEREMONY_EFFECTIVE is the ONE
  2825	  # resolution of the ceremony in this script (recorded install-state, then
  2826	  # --ceremony, then CEO_UPGRADE_CEREMONY, then the fail-safe `user`); this
  2827	  # function CONSUMES it. Only the literal `maintainer` selects the wider
  2828	  # template; every other value — including unset, which only a harness can
  2829	  # produce — takes the narrower one, the same fail-SAFE direction the
  2830	  # resolution itself chose.
  2831	  #
  2832	  # And INFERRED is not RECORDED (rail round 8, P1). With no readable
  2833	  # install-state and no --ceremony, the resolver above answers `user` only as
  2834	  # a fail-safe for ROOT WRITES (_CEREMONY_PERSIST=0): that population is the
  2835	  # pre-Wave-B historical install whose ceremony nobody knows. Treating that
  2836	  # as a real user profile would withhold every base-only hook from a
  2837	  # historical MAINTAINER — the S328 finding itself, for exactly the adopters
  2838	  # this wave exists to reach — and hand it the user-only advisory switch,
  2839	  # which turns a blocking matcher it already carries into an allow. So the
  2840	  # unknown case gets its own posture: the roster BOTH templates share, derived
  2841	  # at run time (hooks by identity key, settings by key AND value), NAMED, with
  2842	  # the opt-in spelled out. Nothing either profile would refuse, and the 20
  2843	  # registrations the profiles agree on do arrive.
  2844	  local template="" _tpl_posture _tpl_label
  2845	  case "${CEREMONY_EFFECTIVE:-}/${_CEREMONY_PERSIST:-0}" in
  2846	    maintainer/1)
  2847	      _tpl_posture="maintainer"
  2848	      template="$SOURCE_DIR/templates/settings/settings.base.json"
  2849	      _tpl_label="templates/settings/settings.base.json" ;;
  2850	    user/1)
  2851	      _tpl_posture="user"
  2852	      template="$SOURCE_DIR/templates/settings/settings.user.json"
  2853	      _tpl_label="templates/settings/settings.user.json" ;;
  2854	    *)
